            <ArticleTitle>Stress Fracture of the Lateral Femoral Condyle after Total Knee Arthroplasty.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>125-128</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">This is to assess one of the rare complications after total knee replacement and to assess risk factors of failure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">11 patients with varus knee and an average age of 67 years underwent TKA between 2005 and 2013. All patients returned with a sudden sharp knee pain, disability to walk and significant decrease in ROM about 4 to 8 weeks after surgery. Radiographic examination revealed a lateral femoral condylar stress fracture.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">After analyzing the images, we found common characteristics among all patients, which might be attributable to the later fracture including varus deformity&gt;25, femoral component lateralization, and valgus correction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Surgeons should be aware of the risk factors to consider before, during, and after surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
